  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening community adaptability and resilience for displaced and host populations in South SudanStrengthening community adaptability and resilience for displaced and host populations in South Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project is a joint effort between the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) and IOM and aims to strengthen stabilization and resilience efforts in South Sudan, targeting areas significantly impacted by the Sudan crisis and its resulting displacement dynamics. The geographic focus includes Upper Nile State and Juba, both experiencing growing pressure due to increased numbers of displaced populations. These locations were selected based on the scale of returns, which made even more critical working on longer-term stabilization and resilience objectives. Juba, as a key urban centre and major point of return for urban returnees, presents opportunities to implement resilience programming on a larger scale by leveraging the skills and profiles of displacement-affected populations who have relocated, and continue to relocate there. Greater Malakal continues to be a critical point of return where the two agencies can scale-up the already ongoing transition and early-recovery interventions.</narrative>
  </description>
